{
  "extends": "expo/tsconfig.base",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true
  },
  // Build output is not source. Without this list the default include pattern
  // `**/*` pulled `dist-test/` -- what `tsc -p tsconfig.test.json` writes --
  // into the same program as `src/`, and `tsc --noEmit` type-checked the
  // compiler's own output. Two consequences, both of them real failures:
  //
  //   1. `Tuong.tsx` and `tuong.ts` compiled to `Tuong.js` and `tuong.js`, and
  //      the pair took the mobile stage down with TS1149. The source pair is
  //      gone (`tuong.ts` is now `bai-dang.ts`), but the emitted pair was the
  //      messenger, not the fault.
  //   2. A renamed or deleted module leaves its stale `.js` behind, so the
  //      gate stays red on a machine that once built the old tree and green on
  //      a fresh clone. That is a gate whose answer depends on what happened
  //      here yesterday.
  //
  // `node_modules` is repeated because naming `exclude` at all replaces the
  // default list rather than adding to it. `tests/ten-module-trung-chu-hoa
  // .test.mjs` plants a collision in `dist-test/` and requires the compiler to
  // stay silent, so this line cannot rot into a comment.
  //
  // `tests/canary` is excluded for a different reason from the rest: the file
  // in it is *supposed* not to compile. It is the fixture behind
  // `tests/actor-id-bat-buoc.test.mjs`, which compiles it through
  // `tsconfig.canary.json` and requires the failure. Leaving it in this
  // program would turn `npm run typecheck` permanently red.
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "tests/canary",
    "dist-test",
    ".expo-build-check",
    ".expo",
    "dist",
    "web-build"
  ]
}
